From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 19:01:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 053/184] segacd: read ahead, so D: survives the calls the
 desktop makes

Dfree walks the whole file allocation table -- eight CD sectors -- and
the desktop calls Dfree before it opens a window. Every one of those
eight cost a seek, because between one GEMDOS block and the next the
head moves on and the stream is lost. Eight seeks is eight seconds when
it works and eight chances to fail when it does not, so the window
stayed shut with no error shown.

A visit to the drive now takes eight sectors, not one: the sector after
the one just read arrives thirteen milliseconds later for free, because
the drive is already reading forward. Read-ahead never seeks -- one
attempt, and if the stream has moved on we stop -- so it costs nothing
when it misses.

Two smaller things the diagnostic found. Only the first attempt of a
fetch resets the decoder; doing it on every retry tore down the lock
the previous one had just got. And a fill that fails is tried once more
from scratch, because the first read after the drive has been doing
something else is the one that fails and only that one -- written down
as a mitigation, because why a fresh start succeeds where three retries
did not is not yet understood.

CONF_SEGACD_CTEST is the instrument: the GEMDOS calls EmuDesk makes on
a drive icon, printed with their return codes, needing no input. It is
how -11 turned from a mystery into 'Dfree walks the FAT'.
---
